title: 'pallet-xcm: weight asset claiming and precompile XCM input by their real size'
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    Fixes weights and benchmark worst cases for the `claim_assets` extrinsic, the
    `ClaimAsset` and `AliasOrigin` instructions, and the `pallet-revive` XCM precompile.
    Each was benchmarked with an input far cheaper than one a caller can actually supply,
    so all four under-charged.

    What this needs from you:

    - Regenerate your `pallet_xcm` weights. `WeightInfo::claim_assets` now takes the asset
      count `n: u32`, `WeightInfo::weigh_message` takes the message length `n: u32`, and
      `decode_xcm(n)` is new.
    - Override `pallet_xcm::benchmarking::Config::get_assets` if your `AssetTransactor`
      deposits several distinct asset kinds. The default measures a flat per-asset slope,
      which is only sound for single-asset-kind transactors such as relay chains.
    - Override `pallet_xcm::benchmarking::Config::batch_call` if you have a batching
      pallet (`utility.batch` for all in-tree runtimes). Left at its `None` default, the
      `weigh_message` worst case degrades and the precompile under-charges.
    - Work out your own `alias_origin` worst case if your `Aliasers` differ from the setup
      `parachains_common::benchmarking` assumes; its docs spell that setup out.
    - Raise gas limits on contracts forwarding large XCMs through the precompile. The new
      size-based charge is taken before decoding and is not refunded.

    `VersionedAssets` gained `len()` and `is_empty()`.
